    {
      "id": "p0124-b007",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "This new text doesn't really seem to be that different from the original text, but there's a cultural reason behind it: in Japanese, green stoplights are called blue stoplights. And since real-life \"blue\" stoplights and red stoplights are opposites, it makes sense for Sans to swap the two in his overly complicated explanation.",
        "raw": "This new text doesn't really seem to be that different from the original text,\nbut there's a cultural reason behind it: in Japanese, green stoplights are called\nblue stoplights. And since real-life \"blue\" stoplights and red stoplights are\nopposites, it makes sense for Sans to swap the two in his overly complicated\nexplanation.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29317439,
          0.52692143,
          0.889735,
          0.61245714
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.29113406,
          0.52620714,
          0.89177532,
          0.61352857
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          29,
          30,
          31,
          32,
          33
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "新台词乍看与原文没什么不同，但背后有一个文化原因：日语会把绿色交通灯叫作蓝灯。现实中的“蓝灯”与红灯意思相反，因此 Sans 在那番过分复杂的解释里把两者对调，就说得通了。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
